<title>USA: Georgia loses 9,000 jobs in July, labor chief says.</title>
<headline>Georgia loses 9,000 jobs in July, labor chief says.</headline>
<dateline>ATLANTA 1997-08-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Georgia lost 9,000 jobs in July due to seasonal layoffs among non-teaching workers in education, state Labor Commissioner David Poythress said Monday.</p>
<p>Nonfarm employment declined to 3.59 million in July from 3.6 million in June.</p>
<p>&quot;These temporary layoffs occur every summer among workers who don't have 12-month contracts like the teachers do,&quot; Poythress said. &quot;But most of these people hold other jobs, so they have additional sources of income during the summer.&quot;</p>
<p>Growth in most other sectors continues to be strong, Poythress said. Georgia added 6,900 jobs for the year, an increase of 0.2 percent from July of last year.</p>
<p>Metropolitan areas and their job changes from June to July were:</p>
<p>--Albany, up 100 to 59,900.</p>
<p>--Athens, down 2,500 to 70,300.</p>
<p>--Atlanta, down 1,300 to 1.95 million.</p>
<p>--Augusta, down 1,100 to 194,400.</p>
<p>--Columbus, down 1,200 to 112,500.</p>
<p>--Macon, donw 1,300 to 146,900.</p>
<p>--Savannah, down 800 to 131,100.</p>
<p>((--Jane Sutton, 305-374-5013))</p>
